{"created": "2026-08-30T12:31:18.584174+00:00", "modified": "2026-08-30T12:31:19.087009+00:00", "id": 22172098, "conceptrecid": "22172097", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22172098", "conceptdoi": "10.5281/zenodo.22172097", "doi_url": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22172098", "metadata": {"title": "The Reason Runs Again: Content-Addressed Decision Records with Checks a Stranger Can Re-Run", "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22172098", "publication_date": "2026-08-30", "description": "Warrant is a record format in which one decision is one immutable, content-addressed, signed record. Its identity is the SHA-256 of its canonical JSON bytes; its governing policy is pinned by hash; its signature covers a domain-separated message; and one class of justification is a deterministic, budget-bounded computation any reader re-executes offline. A verified record establishes integrity, signature validity under a key, and replay - not that the computation interprets the policy or entails the decision (an explicit non-goal). The paper reports a conformance regime in which independent implementations must agree byte-exactly: Python and Go agree at settlement grade, and a from-scratch Rust verifier agrees at base grade. It includes a 138-vector conformance pack with normative negative batteries, an experimental settlement layer with a partly-negative result, and a model-generated review ledger. NOT peer-reviewed; authored by a language-model maintainer; no independent human review and no clean-room implementation to date.", "access_right": "open", "creators": [{"name": "Glova, Serhii", "affiliation": "Independent", "orcid": "0009-0001-8010-420X"}], "keywords": ["AI agents", "accountability", "provenance", "content-addressed storage", "canonicalization", "Ed25519", "domain separation", "verifiable computation", "EU AI Act"], "related_identifiers": [{"identifier": "https://github.com/s0fractal/warrant", "relation": "documents", "scheme": "url"}, {"identifier": "https://github.com/s0fractal/sigma-glyph"